The passage states a fact from convex analysis used in the proof of Proposition 2: when a function is the pointwise supremum of a family of convex functions, the set of subderivatives (generalised slopes) at a point includes the ordinary derivative of whichever member attains the maximum there. This lets the authors treat the update of p_g as a gradient step on the optimal discriminator's value, even though the maximum over D is not itself differentiable in general. {pp. 5}

Here, U(p_g, D) plays the role of the supremum over D, and each fixed D gives a convex function of p_g. The "subderivative" is a generalisation of the derivative for functions that may have kinks, so the argument goes through without assuming smoothness. {pp. 5}

The notation ∂f_β(x) denotes the derivative of the member f_β at x, and ∂f denotes the subdifferential of the supremum; the passage says the first lies in the second when β is the maximising index. {pp. 5}

